Must-have secretary dashboard:

Money stuff
Maintenance pending/paid with status badges + auto QR for UPI
E-receipts generated instantly, downloadable PDF
Expense tracking: Navratri, repairs, security - amount collected vs spent in real time
Monthly/quarterly collection toggle + overdue reminders

Communication
Grievance board: photo + location + status [open/in-progress/done]
Broadcast to all flats via WhatsApp - no app needed
Notice board for meetings, elections, maintenance schedules

Contacts & utilities
Directory: watchman, plumber, electrician, washing guy - tap to call
CCTV login link for posh buildings
Parking slot mapping + visitor log

Add-on that seals it:
Multilanguage option - Hindi/Gujarati/Marathi button
Flat-wise history so secretary can show “Unit 403 hasn’t paid since March”

That’s it. Anything more and adoption dies. Secretary opens it once a month, residents just get WhatsApp.

It's not unnecessary, it's just a boring problem people actually have. That's why it's overlooked.

Why it works:
Secretary pain is real - Jay's bank runs, cash collections, no compensation. They'd pay to not do that.
Residents won't install = solved by WhatsApp layer. Secretary runs the app, everyone else gets QR + reminder on WA. 5-click thing is key.
₹60/flat/year is invisible. People fight ₹500 subscriptions, not ₹60.

Distribution hack you already nailed: friends → society head → pitch → deliver. Flat referral to friends makes it spread in housing clusters.

Make it stick: The lazy switch is the product. Once society’s bills + contacts + CCTV + grievances are all in one place, nobody wants to move 200 numbers back to 12 different WhatsApp groups.

Only risk: selling to the secretary. They’re usually unpaid, busy, and skeptical of new stuff. Lead with “this cuts your bank trips to zero” not “digital transformation”.
